  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\n   We introduce a multi-resolution optimization strategy with automatic structure discovery and fine-grained adaptation to periodicities. This differs fundamentally from the parent's single-grid method. We use correlation peaks decomposition to better identify and minimize harmful overlaps, and employ a genetic algorithm with mixed-integer crossover to explore discontinuous improvements. The key innovation is adaptive grid sizes and the force-directed shift minimization strategy.\n\n   The approach addresses local optima by:\n   - Expanding search with multiple initial structures (periodic, binary, gradient)\n   - Using mixed-integer optimizations to capture hybrid solutions\n   - Implementing phase-separated processing (coarse to fine)\n   - Introducing novel force-directed optimization (FDO) to handle shift penalties\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n   Objective: Minimize C5 = max_k \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx\n   Subject to: 0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1 and \u222b h(x) dx = 1\n\n   Representation:\n   h(x) described by piecewise constant samples at x_i = i*dx, dx=2.0/n_points\n\n   Search strategy:\n   - Initialization from periodic seed, binary seed, and gradient seed\n   - Progressive grid enhancement from n_min to n_max\n   - Hierarchical structure discovery with autocorrelation analysis\n   - Force-directed overlap minimization\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n   a. Inputs:\n      - n_min=40, n_max=1000\n      - min_grid_step logic relevant above)\n\n   b. Initialization (Section 3a-b)\n   c. Core optimization (Section 3c):\n      - For grid size n from n_min to n_max:\n        - Try 3-4 distinct initializations\n        - Apply correlation peak detection\n        - Apply gradient-based or force-directed refinement\n\n   d. Structured phases:\n      - Phase 1: Periodic/gradient initializations\n      - Phase 2: Binary initialization (make_binary_start)\n      - Phase 3: Auto-correlation structure phase\n      - Phase 4: Bi-level optimization (coarse grid, fine grid)\n\n   e. Helper libraries: numpy, scipy.optimize for direct methods (SLSQP, COBYLA)\n\n   f. Detailed callbacks:\n      **force_direct_opt:**\n        - Detect overlap peaks (matching on 1-h(x+k))\n        - Calculate repulsive forces from peak harmonics\n        - Implement time-adaptive step sizes\n\n   g. Deadline management:\n      - Use absolute_remaining_time calculation\n      - Compute phase duration from EMA budget prediction\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n   - Primary initialization choices: periodic_start, binary_start, gradient_start\n   - Correlation threshold for structure detection: 0.002 (relative)\n   - Refinement methods: \n     - 'gradient': If grid > 500, use local gradient descent (L-BFGS-B)\n     - 'force-directed': Otherwise use force-directed optimization (FDO)\n   - Grid search strategy:\n     - Level 1: n=40 (base)\n     - Level 2: n=80,160,320 (quintupling)\n     - Level 3: n=500,1000 (final)\n\n   - Initializations:\n     1. Periodic_start (structured)\n     2. Binary start (random)\n     3. Gradient start (differentiated)\n\n   - Overlap detection threshold: 0.002 (\u00d7conservative)\n   - Projection steps: Whenever h[.] violates [0,1] bounds\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n   - Primary phase identification expects ~30% of total time\n   - Grid point counts: 40,80,160,320,500,1000\n   - Time budget: 1100 seconds total\n   - Memory: 2GB (numpy arrays)\n\n   Queries handled via adaptive sampling: \n   - Schedule updates at budget milestones: n=40,80,160,320,500,1000\n   - Scale down grid sizes when outliers detected\n\n   - Deadline check: At phase boundary (alpha adjustment)\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n   a. Feasibility checks: h[.] \u2208 [0,1] and sum(h)=n/2\n   b. Objective validation: Compute C5 via original correlation mode='full'\n\n   c. Return sequence:\n      - If best solution found, return (h_values, best_c, n_points)\n      - If interrupted, return best solution found so far\n      - If no solution, return the fallback initial_h_values\n\n   d. Projection error handling:\n      - Allow h[.] tolerance violation of 3.0 (absolute) but clip after\n      - Calculate projection diff error to safety margin\n\n   e. Interpolator details:\n      - Original grid preserved in initial iterations\n      - Final sample rates: 40,80,160,320,500,1000\n      - Projection using robust thresholds\n\n   - Stop conditions:\n     1. Feasibility: The function must be [0,1] clamped\n     2. Time: When remaining_time * 1.5 < 60\n     3. Progress: No improvement for 3 consecutive gridups\n\n   f. Score validation: Compute C5 with parent's method\n\nAt the core a multi-resolution optimization is used with hybrid initialization and adaptive structure detection, focusing on radically different approaches than the parent method to achieve a new record.",
